00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.110
&gt;&gt; CARLA A. HILLS: Welcome to this Council
on Foreign Relations meeting.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.110
We are so very honored to have the president
of Mexico, Felipe Calderon.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.110
And before we get started, let me say that
this meeting is on the record, and it is being

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:31.110
telecommunicated to New York by video conference.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.390
So we beg you, turn off all wireless devices
-- phones, BlackBerrys -- not to vibrate but

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.100
off, really off.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:44.120
And as it is customary to preserve the maximum
time for our conversation with the president,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.500
my introduction will be undiplomatically short.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:55.410
You have the president's resume in your papers
that were given to you as you entered the

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:01.930
hall, so let me simply say that President
Calderon, the youngest of five boys, earned

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:09.430
a bachelor's degree in law, master's degree
in economics and a master's degree in public

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:14.720
administration at the John Kennedy School
up at Harvard University.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:25.190
He became a supporter of the party for -- the
National Action Party, PAN, early on, becoming

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.430
the president of the youth organization.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.510
In the late '90s he became its president.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:41.119
And before the 2006 election, as president
of Mexico, he had served as a deputy in Mexico's

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.159
federal chamber of deputies and as secretary
of energy.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:53.159
He will leave office in December, remembered
as the president who built the most universities,

00:01:53.159 --> 00:02:03.409
96; the 16,000 kilometers of highways; a bridge
that connects Mexico's two costs, providing

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:09.769
faster access and therefore more efficient
trade; and the passage of the first employment

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:16.370
act, which provides incentives for companies
to hire people just entering the workforce.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:23.300
He also faced the daunting challenge of the
violence spawned by the drug cartels that

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.200
left 50,000 people dead.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:35.260
A poll taken just this past August showed
his approval rating above 64 percent.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.230
We are so honored to have the President of
Mexico here with us.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:46.360
And, Mr. President, we welcome you to this
podium and look forward to your remarks.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.590
[Applause.]

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:59.730
&gt;&gt; PRESIDENT FELIPE CALDERON: Good morning.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.060
Carla, thank you for your words.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:10.959
Well, it's an honor to be here today at the
Council on Foreign Relations.

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:17.129
For more than 90 years, this institution has
been at the forefront of analysis to understand

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:24.630
the challenges and foreign policy choices
facing the United States and the world.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:34.069
Six years ago, at my first -- (inaudible)
-- address, I told the Mexican people that

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:37.410
it was possible to transform Mexico.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:43.519
I said that we could transform Mexico into
a more prosperous nation with a dynamic and

00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:44.760
competitive economy.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.360
I said that we could be a fair society with
more opportunities for everyone.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:59.240
I was convinced, as I am now, that we could
transform Mexico into a safer nation with

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.180
a strong rule of law.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:10.180
I can tell you today that we have made great
strides and have put Mexico on track to making

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.970
this vision a reality.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:17.690
I would like to share some reflections on
how we have transformed Mexico into a stronger

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:22.249
nation and a better neighbor.

00:04:22.249 --> 00:04:27.139
Let me start by talking about the transformation
of the Mexican economy.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:36.650
As you know, we had to confront the worst
global economic crisis in living memory.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:42.349
Mexico was particularly vulnerable to this
shock because of our ties to the American

00:04:42.349 --> 00:04:49.080
economy and the consumption in this country
which was -- which was the epicenter of the

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.020
crisis three years ago.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:05:00.580
Fortunately, we took the necessary measures
to prevent this crisis from becoming a major

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.580
catastrophe.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:13.029
In doing so, we were guided by three basic
concepts: financial discipline, economic freedom

00:05:13.029 --> 00:05:14.810
and increased competitiveness.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:20.219
And this strategy has paid off to the country.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:26.469
First, Mexico has preserved sound economic
fundamentals.

00:05:26.469 --> 00:05:32.399
Many countries put in place counter-cyclical
measures to protect their economies from the

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:37.969
shock waves or the financial turmoil, and
we did as well.

00:05:37.969 --> 00:05:44.319
In Mexico, we implemented counter-cyclical
measures to save jobs and keep our industries

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:45.319
afloat.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:52.930
For instance, we created specific programs
to save jobs in export-oriented industries.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:06:00.300
We reached an agreement with the unions and
the companies in the sense that as long the

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:10.710
worker accept to earn one-third less on his
salary, and the company accept to pay one-third

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:16.749
of his salary, the government -- the federal
government accepted to pay the other third

00:06:16.749 --> 00:06:24.610
part of his salary, and in that way, we save
more than half a million jobs right in the

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:29.229
middle of the crisis in export-oriented industries.

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:34.979
We increased government transfer to the poorest
and established a temporary jobs program.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:42.819
In doing so, we created another half a million
temporary works for the people.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:47.770
And of course, all this cost a lot of money.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:56.699
Our public deficit rates levels of almost
4 percent of GDP in 2010, and we understood

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:04.469
that today's deficit are tomorrow's taxes,
and the market always punish fiscal lack of

00:07:04.469 --> 00:07:07.399
responsibility.

00:07:07.399 --> 00:07:14.279
That is why expanding public deficit to stimulate
economy is a one-shot deal, and we understood

00:07:14.279 --> 00:07:15.279
that.

00:07:15.279 --> 00:07:23.690
Once you do it, you have to take measures
to rebuild your financial equilibriums.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:31.709
The big mistake made by many economies during
the crisis was to forget that you can use

00:07:31.709 --> 00:07:34.229
public deficit, like this, once.

00:07:34.229 --> 00:07:40.740
But if you try to use it on a regular basis,
you will face, sooner or later, tremendous

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:46.399
problems to finance such deficit and that's
exactly what is happening with several economies

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:49.050
in Europe right now.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:55.889
That is why we put in place an exit strategy
to regain a fiscal balance quickly, and this

00:07:55.889 --> 00:08:05.179
strategy included several painful measures:
for instance, increasing tax revenues, reducing

00:08:05.179 --> 00:08:14.259
public expenditures, promoting the structural
reforms and an aggressive deregulation program.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:21.619
We also shut down a very inefficient energy
company, Luz y Fuerza del Centro, which saved

00:08:21.619 --> 00:08:27.169
the government more than $5 billion a year
in subsidies.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:35.539
And thanks to fiscal discipline, public debt
is at a historically low level, and our foreign

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:44.380
reserves are above $160 billion, more than
twice the amount of our total foreign debt,

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:52.060
and we have maintained the lowest inflation
levels of the past seven decades.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:58.680
Another important factor behind Mexico's stability
is our financial sector.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:06.230
While in the U.S. the crisis started in the
financial markets and in the banking sectors,

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:13.700
Mexico, the financial sector was not part
of the problem, but part of the solution.

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:19.889
Thanks to the improvements in regulations,
Mexican banks have a capitalization index

00:09:19.889 --> 00:09:30.009
of 16 percent, almost double that the recommendation
of Basel, and well above the countries such

00:09:30.009 --> 00:09:35.350
as China, England or even the U.S.

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:44.670
Second, we maintain our strong commitment
to free trade.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.029
This is easier said than done.

00:09:47.029 --> 00:09:55.580
A lot of you remember at every G-20 summit,
one of the useful paragraphs in the final

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:05.899
declaration always say that all countries
pledge to refrain from adopting protectionist

00:10:05.899 --> 00:10:07.740
measures.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.399
All the countries used to say, we reject protectionism.

00:10:11.399 --> 00:10:20.019
And time and again, 15 out of the 20 members
put such measures into effect in the following

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:22.019
days.

00:10:22.019 --> 00:10:27.830
Only Mexico -- only Mexico started to reduce
tariffs right in the middle of the crisis.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:38.420
Mexico, on the contrary, has reduced tariffs
from 12 percent 4 percent on average and entered

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:44.940
into new free trade agreements, such as the
Alliance of the Pacific with Colombia, Chile

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:46.230
and Peru.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:53.800
And a few months ago, Mexico joined the Trans-Pacific
Partnership, TPP, negotiations.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:11:01.389
This will give Mexican trade the biggest boost
since NAFTA came into effect.

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:07.250
Third, we work hard to increase our competitiveness.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:12.890
Despite the crisis, we boosted investment
in infrastructure.

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:22.320
To give you an idea, the 20,000 kilometers
of road we have built and repaired are more

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:28.199
or less equal to the distance between the
North and South Poles.

00:11:28.199 --> 00:11:35.350
We also doubled our bet in our own people
by building -- 96 was the figure last year

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:40.510
-- today is 140 new public universities.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:48.710
And today more than 113,000 engineers graduate
each year in Mexico, a higher figure than

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.079
in Germany, Canada or Brazil.

00:11:51.079 --> 00:11:57.449
And what are the results of these strategies?

00:11:57.449 --> 00:12:06.380
Today Mexico exports more manufacturing goods
than all the countries of Latin America and

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:10.690
the Caribbean combined, including Brazil.

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:19.740
Mexico now accounts for almost 13 percent
of U.S. imports of manufactured goods, and

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:25.680
in 2005, Mexico's share was below 10 percent.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:32.990
Ten years ago about 90 percent of the country's
export went to the U.S.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:40.019
And last year that figure fell to 76 percent.

00:12:40.019 --> 00:12:48.370
When I took office six years ago, Mexico was
the ninth-largest car exporter to the world,

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:54.709
and today we are the fourth-largest car exporter.

00:12:54.709 --> 00:12:59.779
Mexico is now the second-largest importer
of American products in the world, and we

00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:06.589
buy more U.S. goods than the rest of Latin
America combined, more than Japan and China

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:08.689
together.

00:13:08.689 --> 00:13:17.269
Indeed, this deep transformation of the Mexican
economy is of critical importance to the economic

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:22.470
success of the United States as well.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:29.650
But if you -- if we want to guarantee our
mutual prosperity in the long run, we need

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.750
to do more.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:39.069
We need to maintain North American competitive
edge over other regions, and the key to get

00:13:39.069 --> 00:13:43.529
there is more integration, not less.

00:13:43.529 --> 00:13:52.170
NAFTA was a very important step, but our economies
must work to deepen our ties fuller and build

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.040
on this foundation.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:03.480
A second deep transformation that is occurring
in Mexico: that we are becoming a middle-class

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.199
nation.

00:14:05.199 --> 00:14:12.689
Of course, this does not mean that we have
ended poverty once and for all.

00:14:12.689 --> 00:14:21.180
But it means is that the average Mexican families
now has a much better quality of life than

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.930
it did a decade ago.

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:32.269
According to the scholars Rubio and De la
Calle, in the last years Mexican families

00:14:32.269 --> 00:14:39.870
have improved their access to housing, cars,
durable goods such as home appliances and

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:47.180
computers, health and education services and
entertainment, all of which are staples of

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:50.620
the global middle class.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:15:01.070
Let me -- let me give you, for example, how
public policies have helped to these ends.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:05.920
First, universal health coverage.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:12.730
For my government, access to health services
for everyone is a fundamental human right.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:19.899
This is why we made an unprecedented commitment
to improve health care that has resulted in

00:15:19.899 --> 00:15:23.449
a historical achievement: universal health
coverage.

00:15:23.449 --> 00:15:32.930
And doing so, we have built more than 1,000
new hospitals or clinics and rebuilt more

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:36.910
than 2,000 more.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:40.069
Second, expanded education opportunities.

00:15:40.069 --> 00:15:48.790
Today, for the first time, every child in
Mexico can attend elementary school, and we

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:56.149
have expanded university enrollment from 24
[percent] to 34 percent of young people at

00:15:56.149 --> 00:15:58.860
superior -- at superior level.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:07.040
Third, the stronger social programs.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:14.499
We have in place strong support network programs,
which include conditional cash transfer to

00:16:14.499 --> 00:16:22.180
help the poorest families, Oportunidades,
pensions for the elderly in poor communities,

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:30.800
Setenta y Mas, and day care centers in urban
areas for working mothers attended by other

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:33.890
mothers in town.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:40.259
And fourth, more home ownership.

00:16:40.259 --> 00:16:47.110
Home ownership is a yardstick of economic
performance and of the strength of the middle

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:48.200
class.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:55.089
Thanks to a combination of a stable economy,
a solid financial system and government-supported

00:16:55.089 --> 00:17:01.350
programs, in the last six years, more than
5 million Mexican families were able to buy

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:05.970
their own house at record figures.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:12.900
The improvement of living conditions in Mexico
is having a positive effect in the continent.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:22.699
For instance, according to a Pew Hispanic
Center analysis, net migration from Mexico

00:17:22.699 --> 00:17:32.190
to the U.S. has steadily decreased in recent
years, and finally reached zero in 2010, and

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:36.790
probably became a net output from the U.S.
in 2011.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:43.309
Of course, can be explained by the increase
in border security and the economic downturn

00:17:43.309 --> 00:17:49.600
in the United States, but there is a strong
argument that better opportunity for Mexico

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:54.100
have also been a key factor for this phenomenon.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:18:02.860
So by first time ever, net migration to the
U.S. is zero on average.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:11.840
However, we must not forget that migration
is a natural phenomenon for two countries

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:18.640
that has been going -- has been going on for
as long as the United States and Mexico have

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:23.140
existed and will continue to happen naturally.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.710
Our economies are clearly complementary.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:34.169
As a labor-intensive economy, we have the
ability to supply a capital-intensive economy

00:18:34.169 --> 00:18:38.940
such as the U.S. with qualified and hardworking
Mexican workers.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:46.600
This is why I believe that an agenda for the
future should continue to focus, on ensuring

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:53.320
that migration takes place in a legal, secure
and orderly way, but that the U.S. economy

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:59.740
is to strengthen, and Mexicans do not have
to die while trying to get a job.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:07.270
(Inaudible) -- from the values we share as
neighbors and partners to find solutions to

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:15.919
these common problems, and help the many workers
who today live in the shadows of the society.

00:19:15.919 --> 00:19:22.110
And I want to take this opportunity to recognize,
once again, President Obama's decision begin

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:29.799
to give some young immigrants the opportunity
to continue contributing to the strength and

00:19:29.799 --> 00:19:32.510
vitality of the United States.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:39.510
It's a positive move for both countries that
I applaud.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:45.720
And now I want to talk to you about the third
fundamental change initiated due to my time

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:52.080
in office: the struggle to consolidate the
rule of law in Mexico.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:57.570
I know this is an issue that has captured
headlines in the United States and around

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:05.290
the world, and it has been one of the main
challenges my administration has had to face.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:14.520
Over the years, criminal organization grew
in power until they became stronger than the

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:22.590
local authorities in several places in some
parts of the country, and even before my term

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:29.470
started, violence and crime were major concerns
across many cities in Mexico.

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:37.230
But we decided to face the problem head-on,
with the full force of the state.

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:43.570
We implemented a comprehensive strategy with
three main components.

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:53.049
First, to confront the violent criminal organizations
and to support overwhelmed local governments,

00:20:53.049 --> 00:21:00.000
we deployed the federal police and the armed
forces, giving the states and municipalities

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:07.710
time and room for maneuver in order to rebuild
and strengthen their law enforcement agencies,

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:11.440
especially police corps at local level.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:17.940
Second, and more important, we have strengthened
the federal police by increasing the size

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:22.320
of the force from 6(,000) to 36,000 officers.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:28.029
They are also better trained, better debriefed
and better paid.

00:21:28.029 --> 00:21:30.840
We are helping the states to do the same.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:40.169
And we are vetting any top officers in federal
police and passing through a very important

00:21:40.169 --> 00:21:45.840
and particular set of tests for new members.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:52.830
And we are moving to a more transparent and
efficient judicial system based on oral and

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:58.779
open trials, just like the one you have here
in the United States.

00:21:58.779 --> 00:22:08.010
Third, we are investing heavily in education
and health to tackle the social causes of

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:15.440
crime, opening rehabilitation centers for
addicts and rebuilding public spaces like

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:19.410
parks, soccer fields and community centers.

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:25.630
I have to say that Mexico has not been alone
in this security effort.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:31.330
We have found in the Obama administration
a spirit of renewed cooperation to face common

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:36.770
problems under the principle of shared responsibilities.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:44.149
But there is no doubt that much more must
be done, particularly when I -- when it comes

00:22:44.149 --> 00:22:50.110
to monitoring the financial operations of
criminals' organizations in the U.S. and reducing

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:58.210
American demand for drugs.

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:09.149
We need to have a coordinated on both initiatives
to get to the root of this problem which is

00:23:09.149 --> 00:23:18.010
increasing demand for illegal drugs in the
U.S. As long as this market continues growing,

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:23.769
money will keep flowing to the pocket of the
criminals.

00:23:23.769 --> 00:23:33.230
Of course, the best way will be to reduce
illicit drug demand in the U.S.

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:38.710
But frankly speaking, that is not possible.

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:47.889
All alternative solutions must be considered
to curb the massive profits of criminal organizations.

00:23:47.889 --> 00:23:54.010
That includes market alternatives that prevent
drug trafficking from causing so much violence

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:57.970
and death.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:04.779
There is another problem that has become vital
for the security of Mexico and many other

00:24:04.779 --> 00:24:14.299
nations: halting the uncontrolled sale of
assault weapons to criminal organizations.

00:24:14.299 --> 00:24:21.440
Unlimited access to assault weapons in the
U.S. is a key factor in the current strength

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.360
of criminal organizations.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:35.760
You can see a clear correlation between the
moment in which the assault weapons ban expire

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:45.500
here in the U.S. in 2004, and that is exactly
the moment in which the violence in Mexico

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:47.190
started to grow.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:56.510
And it is clear that the more weapons to have
available, more violence of homicide you will

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:58.740
have in any part of the world.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:04.769
You can see the same phenomenon, whether in
Africa or Central America, during civil wars

00:25:04.769 --> 00:25:05.769
and other phenomenons (ph).

00:25:05.769 --> 00:25:11.400
And that is exactly what is happening in Mexico
as well.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:17.710
During my term, we have seized more than 150,000
guns and weapons.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:24.860
And more than 80 percent of them were sold
in an American gun shop.

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:35.419
And there is nine gun shop in the border with
Mexico per each Wal-Mart in that city.

00:25:35.419 --> 00:25:43.000
For Mexico it is absolutely unfair -- unfair
and offensive that so many lives have lost

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:51.279
because of the -- of this business or proceed
to (swell ?) the profits of the arms industry.

00:25:51.279 --> 00:26:02.270
It is possible to think in a regulation that
what established here in the U.S. in the '90s,

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:08.659
the Assault Weapons Ban, is a problem that
has to be addressed if you want to build a

00:26:08.659 --> 00:26:11.050
safer North America.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:18.600
And finally, I would like to talk about a
profound transformation in our role in the

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:21.870
world.

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:27.440
Mexico is no longer a spectator in global
affairs.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:34.230
We are becoming a nation committed to actively
participating in the solutions of global challenges.

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:44.480
For instance, the COP-16 meeting that Mexico
hosted in 2010 was a milestone in the global

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:48.820
fight against climate change.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:54.640
The agreements reached in Cancun were an important
step towards more effective international

00:26:54.640 --> 00:27:02.990
climate finance mechanism such as the Green
Climate Fund, a Mexican proposal (tying ?) resources

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:08.710
to adaptation and mitigation measures around
the world.

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:18.080
We are also -- we are also the first country
in Latin America to chair the G-20 meeting

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:19.559
in June.

00:27:19.559 --> 00:27:26.559
During the leaders' meeting in Los Cabos this
year, we reached important agreements such

00:27:26.559 --> 00:27:34.690
as the capital injections of over $455 billion
to the International Monetary Fund.

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:41.659
Moreover, the Los Cabos action plan for growth
established commitments to deal with the eurozone

00:27:41.659 --> 00:27:45.870
crisis and to strengthen sustainable economic
growth.

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:55.750
Dear friends, ladies and gentlemen, Mexicans
are working to build a better nation, stronger

00:27:55.750 --> 00:28:03.960
and safer country where the rule of law prevails,
as well as more competitive economy and fairer

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:04.960
society.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:13.409
If Mexico continues on this path of transformation
in the future, it is on the threshold of becoming

00:28:13.409 --> 00:28:18.120
the prosperous nation our citizens wish for
and deserve.

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:31.440
This day and age, however, no nation can succeed
without the support of its strategic partners.

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:38.340
The more Mexico and the United States cooperate,
the more we can build a future our people

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:39.669
rightfully demand.

00:28:39.669 --> 00:28:49.340
Both the U.S. and Mexico, with our new presidential
terms this year, if both our economies are

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:58.139
well on the road to health, the window of
opportunity to strengthen the bilateral relationship,

00:28:58.139 --> 00:29:05.309
the upcoming administrations in Mexico have
a solid foundation on which to build a stronger

00:29:05.309 --> 00:29:10.860
future, and I wish my successor all the best.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:21.149
If I look at how far we have come, I have
never been more confident that Mexico and

00:29:21.149 --> 00:29:27.659
the United States will continue deepening
our integration and strengthening our strategic

00:29:27.659 --> 00:29:32.149
partnership as we face up the challenges of
the 21st century.

00:29:32.149 --> 00:29:39.419
It has been a pleasure to stay here, and of
course I will attend all your questions.

00:29:39.419 --> 00:29:45.440
Thank you very much.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:48.409
[Applause.]

00:29:48.409 --> 00:30:03.049
&gt;&gt; HILLS: Mr. President, we thank you for
those very fine and fulsome remarks.

00:30:03.049 --> 00:30:08.860
I would like to ask you, in view of your interest
in open trade and what you've accomplished,

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:16.539
what do you see as the prospects for United
States, Mexico and Canada in the Trans-Pacific

00:30:16.539 --> 00:30:17.769
Partnership?

00:30:17.769 --> 00:30:26.100
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Well, the point is we must to
understand how important is that for our people

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:27.500
and our economies.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:40.799
And if we can get this agreement, we will
connect our economies to the most prosperous

00:30:40.799 --> 00:30:43.200
region in the world at least in two decades.

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:45.240
That is the idea.

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:56.850
My concern is that there are a lot of protectionist
spirits, I can say that, in local politics

00:30:56.850 --> 00:31:02.200
-- the same as, I suppose, in the United States
and Mexico.

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:10.370
So a lot of people believe that more protected
you are the more prosperity you will have.

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:13.980
I mean, it's exactly the other way around.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:20.570
But for any politician, it's easier to talk
about protection and talk about borders and

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:31.110
barriers than to talk about real freedom,
which you and your constituents will compete.

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:32.259
And that is a serious concern.

00:31:32.259 --> 00:31:43.200
And the problem is the protectionism, the
gulf that appears exactly in the middle of

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.850
the crisis.

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:51.279
So what can be the chance to move forward
in the TPP?

00:31:51.279 --> 00:32:02.700
I have -- one problem that I'm seeing that
the day for a real advance in this process

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:05.610
was delayed one more year.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:14.039
So but I listen in the APEC meeting if there
will be an agreement this year, which was

00:32:14.039 --> 00:32:17.049
exactly what we were expecting.

00:32:17.049 --> 00:32:26.480
And it's necessary to regain momentum in local
societies to reach the goal we are looking

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:27.480
for.

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:34.919
And probably the political campaign hurts
Mexico, but especially the presidential campaign

00:32:34.919 --> 00:32:40.419
here will be a temporary obstacle for TPP.

00:32:40.419 --> 00:32:49.289
But once the Americans decide this important
question, I hope that we can move together,

00:32:49.289 --> 00:32:54.090
common efforts in order to get the TPP probably
next year.

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:59.770
And that measure will open big opportunities
for our trade economy.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:06.840
&gt;&gt; HILLS: You mentioned that the North American
Free Trade Agreement was fine and that we

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:08.770
should build on it.

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:14.940
A lot of people have thought that the three
governments could do more to create cohesion

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:18.549
and competitiveness in the Western hemisphere.

00:33:18.549 --> 00:33:25.179
Is that possible or are there disputes between
us -- like maybe the tomato dispute -- that

00:33:25.179 --> 00:33:32.379
hold us back from accomplishing what we might
aspire to for creating new opportunities?

00:33:32.379 --> 00:33:37.390
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: It will be a permanent battle,
if I can say that.

00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:47.379
And first, let me congratulate you, personally,
for this tremendous achievement which has

00:33:47.379 --> 00:33:52.860
a historical dimension, the NAFTA, several
years ago.

00:33:52.860 --> 00:34:01.240
And I think it is possible and it is necessary
to build upon the NAFTA.

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:06.429
In particular, we need to do several things
at the same time.

00:34:06.429 --> 00:34:10.510
One, we need to improve the infrastructure
at the border.

00:34:10.510 --> 00:34:15.060
So there is a lot of bottleneck on both sides.

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:22.160
I mean, if we are able to rearrange to increase
the competitiveness of the border itself then

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:25.020
the infrastructure will be great.

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:36.090
Second, we should understand that the NAFTA
will provide a lot of opportunities, not only

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:43.380
for Mexican workers and providers but also
for American workers and providers.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:51.380
Actually, there is serious research in which
we can demonstrate the real benefits in terms

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:58.660
of jobs and personal income for any one of
the states here.

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:01.270
So we need to explain to the people what are
those benefits.

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:09.110
But again, the problem that free trade will
face at any time, and in particular in electoral

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:11.300
times, is protectionism.

00:35:11.300 --> 00:35:14.770
And that is the case of the tomato.

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:25.390
So it seems like this election is so competitive
and the idea is that the tomato could vote

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:33.350
and could pick the president -- [laughter]
-- and then look like -- incredible that I

00:35:33.350 --> 00:35:34.350
can say that, no?

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:35.450
[Laughter.]

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:42.550
But let me tell you that Mexican tomato is
very good for American consumers -- [laughter]

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.030
-- are cheaper.

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:47.290
And if you are looking for how to improve
the economy, you need to open the border to

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:49.270
the Mexican tomato, not the other way around.

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:50.270
[Laughter.]

00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:52.671
But if the tomatoes are going to select the
next president, I think this country is in

00:35:52.671 --> 00:35:53.671
problems.

00:35:53.671 --> 00:35:54.671
[Laughter.]

00:35:54.671 --> 00:36:00.530
&gt;&gt; HILLS: Let me ask this last question before
I go to the audience.

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:03.040
We want to keep protections down.

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:05.430
And unfortunately, they are going up.

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:11.440
You mentioned, though, the unfortunate nature
of the tomato problem.

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:19.560
What are the prospects of opening energy so
that the United States and Mexico could collaborate

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:25.570
on developing on a really necessary asset
for our economies?

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:29.180
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Well, that's a important question.

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:39.240
I think that today there is a wind of opportunity
to open a little bit, at least, the energy

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.500
sector in Mexico.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:50.670
And I say this understanding that it's absolutely
necessary because today we have a new challenge

00:36:50.670 --> 00:36:52.910
and a new opportunity as well.

00:36:52.910 --> 00:37:02.860
Let me talk, for instance, about Pemex or
any public company or owned-state company.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:14.060
Its managers are looking for the way in which
they can get more benefits with less resources,

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:16.080
like any other company, probably.

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:27.760
But in this particular case -- as long as
the technology moving ahead, and now technology

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:38.540
are -- it's allowing us to get natural gas
in particular, shale gas, a very cheap way,

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:45.600
the price of natural gas in the North American
region going down dramatically.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:55.420
And that is good for industry because it means
cheaper energy for any industry and any economy.

00:37:55.420 --> 00:38:01.550
You -- probably you can remember that 15 or
10 years ago the price of natural gas was

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:04.060
a serious obstacle for the economy.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:12.000
I remember when I was secretary of energy,
the price of natural gas was, like, 12 (dollars)

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:16.190
or $15, and today the price, probably 2 (dollars)
or $3.

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:19.680
So that is an amazing opportunity for the
region.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:26.750
That means that Mexico could be even more
competitive, because now we have a very high

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:28.810
competitiveness in manufacturing sector.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.680
We have very well-trained workers.

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:34.110
We have more infrastructure.

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:36.980
We have free trade with the United States
and with other nations.

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:40.270
It's a lot of, a set of very many competitive
aspects.

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:43.750
Plus today we will have cheaper natural gas.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:46.150
So we have everything to win.

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:47.170
That is the opportunity.

00:38:47.170 --> 00:38:59.060
But the problem is for Pemex managers with
very few public resources who'll decide what

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:02.680
will be the most profitable activity for the
company.

00:39:02.680 --> 00:39:11.530
You need -- you need to decide either to invest
natural gas with very short marginal revenues

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:16.590
and with a lot of risk because you need a
lot of technology and massive investment,

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:24.851
or to invest in oil, which you could cost
probably 20 (dollars), $30 per barrel, and

00:39:24.851 --> 00:39:28.080
you can sell that barrel at more than $100.

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:35.030
Clearly, Pemex could go towards the oil, even
in the very deep Gulf of Mexico.

00:39:35.030 --> 00:39:43.580
By the way, we discovered last month a new
research, a new field, deepwater -- the Gulf

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:44.860
of Mexico.

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:51.300
It's the first time Mexico had such kind of
discover, at least in 40 years.

00:39:51.300 --> 00:40:01.220
And if we compare the forecast of that, that
discover could imply to double our -- (inaudible)

00:40:01.220 --> 00:40:03.640
-- in a very short time.

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:07.100
But let me go back to natural gas.

00:40:07.100 --> 00:40:16.990
So we need to think how can we open the natural
gas sector in order to promote massive investment,

00:40:16.990 --> 00:40:26.350
and with that we can get more job creation,
more energy efficiency, more natural gas at

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:34.860
very low prices, which means more competitiveness
for industry, cheaper electricity for people

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:37.830
and a lot of benefits.

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:45.980
But in order to do so, we need to change the
legal framework of this matter.

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:50.240
Now, why I am saying that there is a window
of opportunity?

00:40:50.240 --> 00:41:01.240
Because I do believe on that, President-elect
Mr. Pena believes that it is necessary to

00:41:01.240 --> 00:41:04.600
reform the energy sector as well.

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:13.660
Look, you have these political willness to
do something -- energy sector, there is a

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:15.090
window of opportunity.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:17.060
So what is going to happen?

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:23.190
On my side, there is all the political willness
that is required.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:26.280
&gt;&gt; HILLS: Well, we'll go to the audience.

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:28.410
I know that there are questions here.

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:34.010
And I would ask, if you're called upon, to
please state your name and affiliation and

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:36.700
wait for the microphone to come to you.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:38.900
On the aisle there, Richard.

00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:44.460
Is there a mic there?

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:46.410
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:49.030
Richard Downey from the Center for Hemispheric
Defense Studies.

00:41:49.030 --> 00:41:52.100
Mr. President, thank you for your remarks,
and thank you for being here with us at the

00:41:52.100 --> 00:41:54.150
Council on Foreign Relations.

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:59.180
One of the hallmarks of your administration
was, in my view, the very courageous decision

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:01.450
to confront organized crime.

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:05.290
And you talked a little bit about the strategy,
the comprehensive strategy to do that.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:13.080
President-elect Enrique Pena Nieto has talked
about making changes to your strategy.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:18.280
Have you talked to him, or perhaps has your
team talked to his transition team, about

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:23.490
how to make continuity and not lose many of
the gains that you've made during your six

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:25.560
years in office, the way ahead?

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:26.560
Thank you.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:28.190
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Thank you.

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:35.420
Now, first let me express my gratitude because
you framed the question exactly in the way

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:36.420
you did.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:41.960
So it -- we are fighting organized crime,
which is not exactly the same that -- "war

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:47.060
on drugs" is the expression coined here in
the United States several decades ago.

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:50.560
And it's absolutely -- not absolutely, but
it's different.

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:53.730
So our aim is rule of law.

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:57.570
So we have not some kind of obsession against
drugs itself.

00:42:57.570 --> 00:43:03.240
It's rule of law which must prevail in the
country, and according to that, we need to

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:05.980
enforce the law; we need to fight organized
crime.

00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:09.881
And organized crime is linked with several
criminal activities -- of course, drugs, but

00:43:09.881 --> 00:43:16.540
not only and probably not mainly drugs, but
also human traffic and also weapons traffic

00:43:16.540 --> 00:43:23.250
and also -- [inaudible] -- and kidnapping
and a lot of very dark activities.

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:33.450
Second, I respect the decision of president-elect
on any matters, including the strategy for

00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:36.750
security in the country.

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:47.830
Now, let me tell you that I was following
all of the electoral campaign.

00:43:47.830 --> 00:43:58.030
A lot of candidates talked about -- they were
framing the expression in this way: We'll

00:43:58.030 --> 00:44:06.630
continue the effort, we will continue the
strategy, but we will make some changes -- [audio

00:44:06.630 --> 00:44:15.390
break] -- and any candidate go exactly the
same in another way.

00:44:15.390 --> 00:44:26.860
When any candidate was asked, what could be
those changes, at least myself, I could not

00:44:26.860 --> 00:44:32.530
see clearly what were going to be those differences.

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:41.920
So from my understanding -- for instance,
one expression is we will use more intelligence

00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:44.050
and less force.

00:44:44.050 --> 00:44:51.770
That is correct, but what is what is exactly
what we are doing, we are improving the information

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:55.610
and intelligence capacity of the public agencies.

00:44:55.610 --> 00:45:01.770
Actually, we build probably the state of the
art center for intelligence for federal police

00:45:01.770 --> 00:45:03.480
right now.

00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:11.620
We are using -- I told a rumor, a conversation
I had with President Bush when we were talking

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:19.400
about the -- we were framing the Merida Initiative
at the very beginning of my administration.

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:22.580
I was trying to explain exactly that.

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:28.050
We need more intelligence, so we need any
kind of instruments and technology in order

00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:33.900
to be able to prosecute criminals and prevent
crime.

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:40.390
Trying to explain that, I told President Bush,
well, have you ever seen this TV show "24"

00:45:40.390 --> 00:45:42.020
with Jack Bauer and so on?

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:43.020
[Laughter.]

00:45:43.020 --> 00:45:46.660
Because I want all the instruments of this
guy.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:47.660
[Laughter.]

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:49.270
And that is exactly what we are doing.

00:45:49.270 --> 00:45:55.420
So it's quite interesting to see the center
of Federal Police -- several centers we are

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:59.940
building, and the same with the Army, and
the same with the PDR.

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:02.310
So we are improving intelligence.

00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:08.710
Actually, we are sharing intelligence with
the U.S. more than ever.

00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:15.070
That could be one change, but exactly in the
same track with this.

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:19.410
Other could be -- what else?

00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:24.070
We will be more preventive than the reactive.

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:35.650
But in any case, you need to be very active
in any field, no?

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:42.850
And I make some kind of a contrast, or I use
some task force I come in from health services,

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:43.850
no?

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:48.910
Of course, we understand that even in health
services, you need to be -- to be more preventive,

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:56.190
and we are applying a little programs in order
to reduce the obesity in child, for instance,

00:46:56.190 --> 00:46:57.390
and the people.

00:46:57.390 --> 00:47:05.870
But once you have -- (inaudible) -- with diabetes,
you need to apply him a very reactive treatment.

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:09.210
Otherwise, we will -- he will die, no?

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:11.500
But that is exactly what is happening in Mexico.

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:15.360
Of course we need -- and actually, we did
a lot of universities.

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:22.430
Building 140 new public universities from
greenfield is not exactly only a question

00:47:22.430 --> 00:47:24.260
of education.

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:26.840
It's a question of opportunities for young
people.

00:47:26.840 --> 00:47:35.280
And I understand that the day in which Mexico
reach full coverage for any young people in

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:41.440
the country, we could solve a lot of the security
problem.

00:47:41.440 --> 00:47:47.330
So you need to be preventive, yes, and that
is the reason why we are creating education

00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:48.330
opportunities for young people.

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:52.840
But at the same time, you need to face the
criminals in the street.

00:47:52.840 --> 00:48:04.770
You cannot allow that the criminals take the
control of one city, to substitute the authority,

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:10.070
because there is this -- the criminals, the
organized crime is doing some kind of a takeover

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:12.120
of the authority in one city.

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:20.780
They reach to a town or even a city, they
threat or bribe the local authority, including

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:25.280
police corps, so they become the new authority
there.

00:48:25.280 --> 00:48:34.410
So they start to apply their own law, they
started to collect their own taxes, and they

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:37.830
started to use public force.

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:43.110
Actually, there is not other public force
than they public force.

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:46.400
So in that sense, it's a challenge to the
state.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:50.110
Now, you are federal government.

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:56.370
Are you going to wait in which you can build
all the universities in that town, and can

00:48:56.370 --> 00:49:01.510
you wait several years until (all the kids
back ?) to those universities?

00:49:01.510 --> 00:49:05.420
How many years is going to take that, and
what is going to happen with the people during

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:07.490
those 10 or 20 or 30 years?

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:10.310
So you need to defend the families.

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:13.600
There is no option to act.

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:19.840
And that is the problem, I think, that Mexico
suffered a lot, because this lack of action

00:49:19.840 --> 00:49:26.940
of the government allow the criminals to take
over a lot of regions.

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:31.590
And it's not limited -- that is not only a
question that the government started fighting

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:35.390
the criminals and that then the violence went
up.

00:49:35.390 --> 00:49:38.750
Of course there is a relationship, yes.

00:49:38.750 --> 00:49:46.540
But frankly speaking, I do believe that the
violence will be there even without the action

00:49:46.540 --> 00:49:47.870
of the government.

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:48.930
Why?

00:49:48.930 --> 00:49:54.200
Because we are now in the face of a new criminal
phenomenon.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:50:01.540
In the past, last century, what was the core
business of the criminals?

00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:03.280
Narco-traffic.

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:05.580
Even -- that is the -- what the words mean.

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:09.990
Traffic -- the U.S., and that's it.

00:50:09.990 --> 00:50:13.360
And it's a problem of calculus, if I can say
that.

00:50:13.360 --> 00:50:14.960
It's only one dimension.

00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:22.820
If you think in a line crossing from the Pacific
sea to the American border, it's one line,

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:27.670
one highway and one point -- single point
in the border.

00:50:27.670 --> 00:50:30.430
But what happen in the last two decades?

00:50:30.430 --> 00:50:36.160
The criminals who start to think in another
business additional to narco-traffic, which

00:50:36.160 --> 00:50:43.280
is drug -- the retail drug -- retailing in
Mexico, which is different why?

00:50:43.280 --> 00:50:48.540
How many people do you need in order to cross
one ton on cocaine -- of cocaine to United

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:49.540
States?

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:51.440
I don't know, 10 people?

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:52.440
Twenty?

00:50:52.440 --> 00:50:53.440
Maybe 50?

00:50:53.440 --> 00:50:54.440
Maybe 100?

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:55.440
OK.

00:50:55.440 --> 00:51:02.990
But how many people do you need in order to
distribute 1 million bags of one gram of cocaine,

00:51:02.990 --> 00:51:05.650
which is the same ton of cocaine?

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:12.390
You will need hundreds or maybe thousand of
people -- a lot of people distributing.

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:13.930
It's absolutely different business.

00:51:13.930 --> 00:51:21.190
And upon that, you need to think that in the
old business, if I can say that, they only

00:51:21.190 --> 00:51:24.950
need to care about one line towards the United
States.

00:51:24.950 --> 00:51:31.930
But if they are starting to go into the retailing
business, they need to cover the whole territory.

00:51:31.930 --> 00:51:33.910
That's like any retailing.

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:36.040
Think about Coca-Cola -- (inaudible) or think
about PepsiCo.

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:38.550
It's exactly the same.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:40.950
They are trying to cover a territory.

00:51:40.950 --> 00:51:48.150
And in order to cover a territory, they started
to clash, to shock one each other.

00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:55.340
And this disputing over territory is the origin
of violence; it's not the action of the government

00:51:55.340 --> 00:51:56.610
itself.

00:51:56.610 --> 00:52:03.360
In other words, when our economies have started
to grow -- for instance -- (inaudible) -- Mexican

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:10.750
income per capita went up from $2,000 a year
to more than $10,000 a year today.

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:16.140
And that implies that Mexican market has started
to be a very important consumption market

00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:21.720
for vehicles, for households, for whatever,
including drugs.

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:25.630
So they started to see our society as a market
as well.

00:52:25.630 --> 00:52:32.610
And then they started to diversify their activity
from only exporting, if I can say that, to

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:33.610
distributing.

00:52:33.610 --> 00:52:39.600
And in this change, they started to fight
each other for the territories.

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:45.390
And that clash, that battle explains 80 percent
of the violence, probably, because it's a

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:51.730
battle between the Zetas against the Pacifico;
or now the Zetas split up, so now it's the

00:52:51.730 --> 00:52:56.940
Zetas against they -- their old allies, which
is the Gulfo.

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:58.350
And they are clashing each other.

00:52:58.350 --> 00:53:03.790
And today probably there is another fight
inside the Zetas itself.

00:53:03.790 --> 00:53:06.690
So the problem is this expansion.

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:11.100
Now, the question was very simple -- [chuckles]
-- but I'm sorry for my answer.

00:53:11.100 --> 00:53:12.100
[Laughter.]

00:53:12.100 --> 00:53:14.280
I use -- I use the opportunity only.

00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:21.130
But let me tell you, I do believe that next
administration -- at least exactly what they

00:53:21.130 --> 00:53:25.520
are saying -- they are going to continue the
effort.

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:33.190
And I think they have the right and the duty,
I suppose, to analyze another alternatives.

00:53:33.190 --> 00:53:37.240
Now, let me be honest.

00:53:37.240 --> 00:53:42.930
You really want to think about a real -- another
alternative?

00:53:42.930 --> 00:53:51.480
I cannot see any one different from to think
about the regulation of drugs in the global

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:54.780
market, starting here in the U.S.

00:53:54.780 --> 00:54:00.920
That is reason why I say if we are not able
or you are not able to reduce the demand for

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:09.480
drugs in a dramatic way, you need to think
in different alternatives.

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:16.100
And you have the duty to analyze any other
alternatives in order to stop the flow of

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:21.240
money towards the hands of the criminals.

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:27.720
And that implies to analyze even market alternatives
for that, because if you want to talk about

00:54:27.720 --> 00:54:32.750
the real different way to do these things,
that is the only one.

00:54:32.750 --> 00:54:39.790
Or the other is to return the power to the
criminals, to say, I won't fight with them

00:54:39.790 --> 00:54:41.070
anymore.

00:54:41.070 --> 00:54:42.670
Please be my guest.

00:54:42.670 --> 00:54:44.940
Take -- you like this government?

00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:46.750
Please take this government.

00:54:46.750 --> 00:54:47.840
You like that city?

00:54:47.840 --> 00:54:53.920
It's nice, so -- [laughter] -- I like it so
much, but take it; I won't fight.

00:54:53.920 --> 00:55:00.750
You cannot say that like a government, because
it's the first duty -- the first duty of any

00:55:00.750 --> 00:55:07.100
government is to preserve the right of the
security of the families.

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:10.640
And that is very top duty.

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:14.730
Believe me, I know that.

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:18.820
But you must do that.

00:55:18.820 --> 00:55:21.270
&gt;&gt; HILLS: Yes, please.

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:22.270
Microphone.

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:24.550
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: [In Spanish.]

00:55:24.550 --> 00:55:27.880
My name is Christopher Broughton; I work with
the Millennium Challenge Corporation.

00:55:27.880 --> 00:55:35.340
I have a related question, and that is the
movement of arms and bulk cash to Mexico from

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:36.880
the United States.

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:42.330
What has your administration done to strengthen
Mexico's own border security to prevent these

00:55:42.330 --> 00:55:43.330
illegal flows?

00:55:43.330 --> 00:55:44.330
Thank you.

00:55:44.330 --> 00:55:51.530
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Well, talking about weapons,
we are siezing a lot, no?

00:55:51.530 --> 00:55:58.210
One hundred and fifty thousand guns and weapons
in five years is a lot.

00:55:58.210 --> 00:56:04.700
And we are establishing some kind of controls
on the highways.

00:56:04.700 --> 00:56:08.410
We are working really hard on that field.

00:56:08.410 --> 00:56:17.610
Talking about stemming illegal cash, that's
more difficult for us.

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:23.140
For instance, we were estimating that three
years ago -- two years ago, only in the banking

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:29.290
sector we detected, like -- we could be, like,
14 billion (dollars) -- 14 billions (dollars)

00:56:29.290 --> 00:56:40.940
a year, which it was dollars that we needed
to send back to the United States coming from

00:56:40.940 --> 00:56:43.860
the banking system.

00:56:43.860 --> 00:56:48.410
And we had not cleared the sources of those
dollars, no?

00:56:48.410 --> 00:56:51.880
So that made us to take a lot of measures.

00:56:51.880 --> 00:56:57.900
For instance, we forbidden a lot of cash transfers
in dollars in Mexico.

00:56:57.900 --> 00:57:03.350
And of course, I got a lot of reject, especially
in border towns.

00:57:03.350 --> 00:57:09.320
I need to imply a lot of political -- (inaudible)
-- for my own party, but it was necessary.

00:57:09.320 --> 00:57:16.590
And we are reducing dramatically the kind
of not well-explained dollars in the banking

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:21.180
system probably towards 4 billion (dollars)
-- 4 billion (dollars) a year today, so we

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:23.600
are reducing that part.

00:57:23.600 --> 00:57:32.920
The other, I presented to the Congress a bill
in order to regulate laundry money.

00:57:32.920 --> 00:57:39.230
Well, probably that was, like, two or three
years ago.

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:40.400
(Inaudible.)

00:57:40.400 --> 00:57:43.000
We are waiting one chamber of the other.

00:57:43.000 --> 00:57:44.001
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: (Inaudible) -- the Senate?

00:57:44.001 --> 00:57:45.001
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Today it's in the Senate.

00:57:45.001 --> 00:57:48.900
Probably tomorrow will be again in the Chamber
of Deputies, and after tomorrow will be again

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:56.130
in the Senate, but we are expecting an approval
from the -- well, secretly what I hope, and

00:57:56.130 --> 00:58:01.780
there is a new session and a new legislation
in the Congress.

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:08.980
Actually I really appreciate that there are
four senators here with me today from different

00:58:08.980 --> 00:58:10.440
political parties.

00:58:10.440 --> 00:58:17.110
And I think that there is a very good chance
that we can finally an approval on this very

00:58:17.110 --> 00:58:19.920
important law in order to fight laundry money.

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:23.780
There are a lot of interests against the law.

00:58:23.780 --> 00:58:28.510
For instance, we are asking to the people,
you cannot pay in cash.

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:38.300
For instance, when you buy a house, you need
to pay by a check, which is obvious of my

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:43.010
understanding, a lot of things, but some people
don't like that.

00:58:43.010 --> 00:58:44.010
[Chuckles.]

00:58:44.010 --> 00:58:46.450
But it's absolutely necessary.

00:58:46.450 --> 00:58:47.680
&gt;&gt; HILLS: I think we have time for one more
question.

00:58:47.680 --> 00:58:51.020
Let me come to this side of the room.

00:58:51.020 --> 00:58:56.450
Yes, please, the -- wait for the mic.

00:58:56.450 --> 00:59:00.240
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: Margaret Daly Hayes of Evidence
Based Research.

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:05.220
Thank you very much, Mr. President, for your
very encouraging remarks about progress in

00:59:05.220 --> 00:59:08.680
Mexico.

00:59:08.680 --> 00:59:14.290
You are very proud of opening a large number
of universities.

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:21.440
Can you talk a bit about what Mexico is doing,
needs to do, to expand and improve primary

00:59:21.440 --> 00:59:25.540
and secondary education in order to enhance
competitiveness?

00:59:25.540 --> 00:59:26.540
Thank you.

00:59:26.540 --> 00:59:37.370
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: That is very, very important,
but let me talk about what we did.

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:44.800
We reached an agreement with a very important
player, which is the union of teachers.

00:59:44.800 --> 00:59:52.740
Union of teachers is a small organization
with probably 1.5 million members in the country.

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:53.740
[Laughter.]

00:59:53.740 --> 00:59:59.210
And we reached an agreement in order to improve
the quality of the education.

00:59:59.210 --> 01:00:10.660
There are several measures that we agreed
and that were -- those were first, in the

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:22.750
past, a post of teacher was decided by a finger
point, either the union or the governor.

01:00:22.750 --> 01:00:31.200
Today for any post of teacher, any position
of teacher, it's decided by a contest between

01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:32.310
teachers.

01:00:32.310 --> 01:00:39.900
So today you want to get a position as teacher,
you need to compete with other teachers in

01:00:39.900 --> 01:00:43.240
order to select the best of them.

01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:47.800
And that is a (structural ?) reforming education.

01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:54.020
Of course, it's a long way to do that, but
there is almost 100,000 new teachers appointed

01:00:54.020 --> 01:00:59.870
in that way -- and does imply a very painful
process.

01:00:59.870 --> 01:01:10.830
For instance, we saw several strikes of teachers
in several states, but we, that measure is

01:01:10.830 --> 01:01:14.360
on track and it's a good one.

01:01:14.360 --> 01:01:21.390
Second, we are establishing an -- (inaudible)
-- waiting process for students and for teachers

01:01:21.390 --> 01:01:22.410
as well.

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:26.570
And of course, there are a lot of -- a lot
of resistance for that.

01:01:26.570 --> 01:01:33.510
But finally, we got special exams in order
to analyze the level of the teacher.

01:01:33.510 --> 01:01:38.240
And we are explaining, it's not a questions
of remove that teacher; it's a question of

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:47.500
we need to see your weakness in order to help
you to improve your capacities as teacher.

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:53.300
Third, we improve the infrastructure in the
schools.

01:01:53.300 --> 01:01:58.290
It's not a matter of federal government, by
the way, because let me tell you, education,

01:01:58.290 --> 01:02:03.740
as health, is a matter of local level of government
since the '90s.

01:02:03.740 --> 01:02:13.730
However, we understood that local government,
probably they have not enough money or they

01:02:13.730 --> 01:02:19.640
have another priority different from improve
the quality of the schools, elementary schools.

01:02:19.640 --> 01:02:27.240
So we started to rebuild, you know, the window,
the basketball field, the bathrooms for girls

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:28.750
and so on.

01:02:28.750 --> 01:02:35.690
And in that sense we have rebuilt 50,000 elementary
schools, very small public works, but we are

01:02:35.690 --> 01:02:37.470
doing so.

01:02:37.470 --> 01:02:49.760
Fourth, we are establishing a new system of
payments to the teachers because we are introducing

01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:51.920
economic incentives.

01:02:51.920 --> 01:03:02.850
So if you are teacher, the more your students
improve, the more salary you earn.

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:06.160
So in that sense, we are paying in terms of
the quality.

01:03:06.160 --> 01:03:10.870
(Inaudible) -- a very small piece of the salary,
but it's improving.

01:03:10.870 --> 01:03:18.760
And finally, we reached last year another
very important reform in which so-called Carrera

01:03:18.760 --> 01:03:26.680
Magisterial was mainly oriented to pay to
the teachers in order to -- how many seminars

01:03:26.680 --> 01:03:29.390
you could have.

01:03:29.390 --> 01:03:37.890
For instance, well, I don't want to make some
examples of what kind of course they were

01:03:37.890 --> 01:03:46.020
taking, but today, 50 percent of the Carrera
Magisterial, which is very linked with the

01:03:46.020 --> 01:03:54.810
money they receive, is still 50 percent depends
on the improvement of your own students according

01:03:54.810 --> 01:04:02.650
to the exams we are doing year after year.

01:04:02.650 --> 01:04:12.570
Now, one outcome could be, for instance, the
PISA test.

01:04:12.570 --> 01:04:21.840
In six years we establish a goal in the -- in
PISA test, and we are reaching, we overcome,

01:04:21.840 --> 01:04:24.780
we surpassed our goal in 2009.

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:32.630
And probably we did the same this year, especially
in math.

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:43.780
Mexican students were the students that advanced
the most in PISA exam, in particularly in

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:45.730
math, in 70 -- in 65 countries.

01:04:45.730 --> 01:04:52.291
Of course, Mexico is probably still today
one of the last countries unfortunately on

01:04:52.291 --> 01:04:53.291
that.

01:04:53.291 --> 01:04:56.790
But we are advancing a lot, in particular
in math.

01:04:56.790 --> 01:05:01.560
And we are measuring that in our own exams.

01:05:01.560 --> 01:05:12.840
For instance, the Enlasse (ph) -- it's an
evaluation program we have last year to this

01:05:12.840 --> 01:05:13.840
year.

01:05:13.840 --> 01:05:21.650
(Inaudible) -- our goal was to reach 40 percent
of good at -- excellent students.

01:05:21.650 --> 01:05:26.090
We overcome that goal in that sense in math.

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:27.750
We are not doing the same, unfortunately,
in Spanish.

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:30.550
So we need to improve on that.

01:05:30.550 --> 01:05:39.130
Now, talking about the challenges, I admit
that this is probably one of the most demanding

01:05:39.130 --> 01:05:41.820
challenge for Mexico today.

01:05:41.820 --> 01:05:49.540
I mean, it's absolutely necessary to do much
more in terms of quality of education.

01:05:49.540 --> 01:05:54.630
And you need to get more cooperation coming
from teachers unions.

01:05:54.630 --> 01:06:04.860
The paradox is there are two kinds of teachers
unions.

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:09.990
One has a lot of bad practices, if I can say
that, or bad reputation.

01:06:09.990 --> 01:06:15.550
But the other one could be worse.

01:06:15.550 --> 01:06:17.080
[Laughter.]

01:06:17.080 --> 01:06:24.040
So one is a lot of things that we need to
improve -- and that is, for instance, a new

01:06:24.040 --> 01:06:31.760
bill I presented to the Congress last month
in order to reform the labor law, to open

01:06:31.760 --> 01:06:39.970
to transparency the finances of the union,
and to stop this direct vote and universal

01:06:39.970 --> 01:06:43.620
vote in the union and so on.

01:06:43.620 --> 01:06:49.140
But there is another group -- another union
more radical.

01:06:49.140 --> 01:06:51.440
They refuse exams.

01:06:51.440 --> 01:06:56.170
They take the control even of the payroll
of the workers.

01:06:56.170 --> 01:07:02.380
So the -- there are some governments in Mexico
that they need to give the money to the union

01:07:02.380 --> 01:07:09.430
teachers and the union pays the salaries according
to its own criteria.

01:07:09.430 --> 01:07:17.130
So unfortunately it's my own state, Michoacan,
but it's -- that's a probably in that.

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:20.030
&gt;&gt; HILLS: Mr. President, we could go on all
morning.

01:07:20.030 --> 01:07:22.217
We really do thank you for your presence,
but also your excellent, wide-ranging remarks.

01:07:22.217 --> 01:07:23.217
[Applause.]

01:07:23.217 --> 01:07:24.217
Let me make a request -- let me make a request
of the audience, would you please out of courtesy

01:07:24.217 --> 01:07:25.217
stay seated for just one minute and a half
so the president can exit with his party and

01:07:25.217 --> 01:07:26.217
get to his next appointment on time -- just
a minute and half.

01:07:26.217 --> 01:07:27.217
&gt;&gt; CALDERON: Don't worry -- [inaudible, laughter].

01:07:27.217 --> 01:07:28.217
&gt;&gt; HILLS: You have to run fast.

01:07:28.217 --> 01:07:29.217
Thank you so much.

01:07:29.217 --> 01:07:30.217
[Applause.]

01:07:30.217 --> 01:07:31.217
